{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when all the men of war… and HaShem spoke to me.</b> Rashi explained: from the time of the sending of the spies, the [divine] speech had not been addressed to me exclusively. And the question is raised here: but surely soft speech (אֲמִירָה רַכָּה) indicates affection more than the term דִּבּוּר does, which indicates harshness. And I say: it is precisely this that proves it — for every דִּבּוּר that is harsh is rebuke and instruction, as in 'אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים' ('these are the words'), because they are words of rebukes, etc.; and therefore they contain greater affection, for 'whom HaShem loves, He rebukes' (Proverbs 3:12) — in contrast to soft speech (אֲמִירָה), which contains no instruction, and therefore does not contain affection to the same degree. For what the Sages termed 'harshness and softness' by the expressions דִּבּוּר and אֲמִירָה is from the standpoint of the matter itself — whether it contains mercy or judgment — but not from the standpoint of the recipient. For when the lover desires the beloved and wants the impression of the speech to be fixed in him so that it never depart from him, he speaks harshly, and the voices and lightnings of the giving of the Torah bear this out, as it says (Exodus 20:17): 'and in order that His fear be upon your faces, so that you do not sin'; but when the love is not so intense, he speaks in faint language and soft speech, and their impression is not fixed as firmly."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:11:25.750298260Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}